 :findings [{:id :synthetic-owner-incompatibility
             :severity :blocking
             :evidence "Actual i3_collector.cljc lines 115-126 require synthetic? true and return authority none; lines 189-203 choose the next head from eight predeclared tokens and treat trusted-fixture? as a caller-supplied boolean. The real desktop_fixture.cljs lines 197-206 describe read-head! as validated byte identity, not authorization; lines 218-262 call the owner after locked validation and return append/result. There is no demonstrated authenticated correspondence between these interfaces."
             :repair "Do not wire a fixed synthetic ACK to real admission or use the P1 lease as real freshness. For the five-file proposal limit the native fixture to independently invoking and verifying the existing authorized S2A owner and evaluating a separate fixture-only replay/comparison; treat P1 as a synthetic model only. Explicitly state this cannot validate real P1-to-owner integration. If integrated collector semantics are required, stop for a separately designed, exact-path and independently reviewed minimal modification/addition ceiling and trusted evidence protocol; no assumed sixth file or fabricated owner proof."}
            {:id :producer-undiscovered
             :severity :blocking-for-plan-correctness
             :evidence "The plan treats a producer as missing, but metadata identifies installed /usr/bin/xmessage from x11-utils. See producer-pin. This establishes a candidate, not successful X11 behavior or lifecycle control."
             :repair "Replace missing-producer/install contingency with the exact xmessage candidate pin and inspect its static invocation/lifecycle contract; separately verify execution only under the future native permit. No installation is needed merely because xterm is absent. If a later necessary capability is actually missing, pin and separately authorize only its public package and dependencies."}
            {:id :coverage-vs-quiescent-cut
             :severity :blocking-for-claimed-native-coverage
             :evidence "Plan lines 21-23 and 31-33 acknowledge unverified distro-source equivalence, event coverage and X completion, but the positive matrix can be read as demanding a general stream-qualified cut despite those unresolved facts. Two tree captures and quiet ticks do not prove operation completion, unseen mutations, or complete event coverage."
             :repair "Split qualification outcomes: complete event-path and ordering proof for the exact installed build plus controlled tests, versus a narrowly bounded quiescent fixture cut with independently established producer/controller exclusion and completed operations. The latter proves only its fixture cut and comparison, never general event coverage, operational currentness or full S2B. Fail closed if neither proof is available; document scope and exclusions per field and mutation."}
            {:id :stream-schedule-oracle-ambiguity
             :severity :major
             :evidence "Lines 31-36 and 46 ask for a pre-generated late event, server-generation witness, two snapshots and a common fence, but do not define where the delayed event sits relative to the matching pre marker or how independent captures and mapping are tied to an exclusion interval. On a demonstrated FIFO stream an event preceding pre cannot arrive after a consumed pre marker without proxy reorder/loss; events before pre may be legitimately incorporated in the subsequent snapshot, not automatically invalidate it."
             :repair "Specify exact generation, stream index and proxy-release schedule for each case. A pre-event delivered before pre is incorporated only after independently proven processing/exclusion; one held past pre violates the FIFO qualification and invalidates it. A relevant event between markers invalidates the candidate; a post-marker delay or drop is a transport fault, never native ordering evidence. Bind both independently requested and parsed GET_TREE snapshots, bounded identity map and owner comparison to the same proven no-writer interval, capture sequence, epoch and fence generation; independently test parser fault and identity-map mismatches. Do not compare separately timed trees as atomic without this proof."}
            {:id :lifetime-and-loss-matrix
             :severity :major
             :evidence "Line 36 correctly denies old identity after uncertain reconnect; line 49 asks for forced same/cross-epoch XID reuse only where achievable, but does not explicitly require negative assertions linking all stale incarnations and accepted-head ambiguity to real owner replay and command refusal. A process/window creation signal alone does not prove a uniquely identified surviving lifetime."
             :repair "Specify tests for close/recreate and reconnect uncertainty using fixture-owned XID plus per-lifetime incarnation; reject stale targets, old memberships and cross-epoch equivalence, preserve historical gap, and require direct causal reconciliation after an actually admitted gap. Keep unknown or late owner results pending until exact owner head and event evidence resolves them. If XID reuse cannot be forced, record native coverage missing and run only separately permitted offline collision schedules; no inferred native reuse."}]
